Example 1:

print "A" @3; print "B"; print "C" after 1

What should happen:

Instruction 2 (B) is independent, so it can finish early.
Instruction 3 (C) must wait for instruction 1 (A) to fully finish (including its 3s delay).
Valid pattern: B appears before A and C, and C always appears after A.

Proof of concurrency:

You see concurrency (B runs while A sleeps) plus a strict dependency edge (1 -> 3).

------------------------------------------------------------

Example 2:

print "FetchData" @2; print "Auth" @1; print "Process" after 1,2; 
print "LogAuth" after 2

Process (3) only appears after both FetchData (1) and Auth (2).
LogAuth (4) only depends on Auth (2), so it may appear before or after FetchData (1).
Valid outcomes include:
Auth, LogAuth, FetchData, Process
Auth, FetchData, LogAuth, Process

Proof of concurrency:

It demonstrates correct multi-parent synchronization

----------------------------------------------------------- 

Example 3:

print "Compile" @2; print "Test" @1 after 1; print "Package" after 2; 
print "Notify"; print "Deploy" after 3,4

Chain: Compile (1) -> Test (2) -> Package (3).
Notify (4) is independent and can happen anytime.
Deploy (5) must wait for both Package (3) and Notify (4).
So 5 is always last among {3,4,5}, and never appears before 3 or 4.

Proof of concurrency:

It shows mixed behavior: a sequential dependency chain running 
concurrently with an independent task, then a final join barrier.

